// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row tuplestruct_door_default_ref_mode_not_carried (tier 1, `run 2`).
// A DOUBLE FREE. Found 2026-09-09f by a counter-example written for the Rust-2024
// binding-modifier round, not by any probe: `match &t { TS(ref a, b) }` was NOT refused by
// a rule that refuses the same thing at every other container door, and the reason turned
// out to be upstream — THE TUPLE-STRUCT PATTERN DOOR DOES NOT IMPLEMENT DEFAULT BINDING
// MODES AT ALL (spec pat.binding.default-by-ref-mode /
// pat.binding.default-mode-carried-into-subpatterns).
//
// PROVEN DIRECTLY, with no destructor involved: under `match &t`, `TS(a, b) => { let x:
// i64 = a; }` COMPILES — `a` is `i64` by value, where every other door binds `&i64`.
//
// THE COST, measured here: with a MOVE-ONLY element the by-value binding is Drop-scheduled
// at arm exit AND the scrutinee — which still owns `t` — drops it again at scope end.
// `D::drop` runs TWICE where Rust runs it once. The count is read through a `static mut`
// AFTER the value's scope has ended, so the wrong answer is an EXIT CODE (2 today, 1 in
// Rust) and not a stdout string a reader could mis-parse.
//
// TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE PATTERN DISAGREE, which is what makes this a defect and not a
// design: measured on the same binary, `match &t { TS { 0: d, 1: k } }` — the struct
// spelling of the SAME tuple struct — runs the destructor ONCE (exit 1), and so does the
// named-field struct `S { a: D, b: i64 }` with `S { a: d, b: k }`. Only the CALL spelling
// `TS(d, k)` double-frees.
//
// INHERITED, NOT INTRODUCED: exit 2 on the base binary 6e10b5ffbceba8b4 (HEAD e8a4c58b1)
// and exit 2 on d903fbec773d7d16, the binary that landed the modifier rule. The modifier
// rule cannot reach this door — rule 2, the doors are in SERIES: the mode must SHIFT
// before "a modifier under a shifted mode" can be an error, and here it never shifts.
// SAME EVIDENCE for the LANDED `mut` third: `match &t { TS(mut a, b) }` also compiles.
package tuplestruct_door_default_ref_mode_not_carried;
use logos.std.fmt;

struct D { v: i64 }
impl Drop for D {
    fn drop(&mut self) { unsafe { COUNT = COUNT + 1i64; } }
}
struct TS(D, i64);

static mut COUNT: i64 = 0i64;

fn main() -> i32 {
    {
        let t: TS = TS(D { v: 7i64 }, 5i64);
        match &t {
            TS(d, k) => { let _z: i64 = d.v; }
        }
    }
    unsafe {
        println!("drops={}", COUNT);
        return COUNT as i32;
    }
}
